---

<BaseLayout {title} {description} {canonical} {schema} lang="en-US">
  <div class="container">
    <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="/finance">Finance</a><span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">How Does Compound Interest Work?</span></nav>

    <div class="page-head">
      <h1>How Does Compound Interest Work?</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">The single most important idea in personal finance — and why starting early beats starting big.</p>
    </div>

    <section class="content-section">
      <p>Compound interest is often called the "eighth wonder of the world," and for good reason: it's the mechanism that turns small, consistent savings into substantial wealth over time. Here's how it works, in plain language.</p>

      <h2>The core idea: interest on interest</h2>
      <p>With <strong>simple interest</strong>, you earn interest only on your original deposit. With <strong>compound interest</strong>, you earn interest on your original deposit <em>plus</em> the interest you've already earned. Each period, your balance grows a little larger, and the next period's interest is calculated on that larger amount.</p>
      <p>That feedback loop is what makes growth <em>accelerate</em> rather than stay linear. In the early years, the effect looks small. In the later years, it dominates.</p>

      <h2>A worked example</h2>
      <p>Suppose you invest <strong>$5,000</strong> at a <strong>7% annual return</strong>, compounded monthly, with no additional contributions:</p>
      <table class="ref-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Balance (approx.)</th><th>Interest earned that year</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1</td><td>$5,361</td><td>$361</td></tr>
          <tr><td>10</td><td>$10,048</td><td>~$680</td></tr>
          <tr><td>20</td><td>$20,193</td><td>~$1,330</td></tr>
          <tr><td>30</td><td>$40,574</td><td>~$2,660</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>Notice how the annual interest keeps growing even though you never added a dollar after the initial $5,000. By year 30, your money has roughly <strong>eight-folded</strong> — without a single additional deposit.</p>

      <h2>The three levers that control your outcome</h2>
      <h3>1. Rate of return</h3>
      <p>A higher rate compounds faster. The difference between 5% and 8% may look small on paper, but over 30 years it can nearly double your final balance.</p>
      <h3>2. Time</h3>
      <p>Time is the most powerful lever, because it gives compounding more periods to work. This is why starting in your 20s beats starting in your 40s — even with a smaller amount.</p>
      <h3>3. Frequency of compounding</h3>
      <p>Interest can compound annually, monthly, daily, or even continuously. More frequent compounding produces slightly faster growth for the same annual rate.</p>

      <h2>The dark side: compounding works against debt, too</h2>
      <p>The same math applies to what you owe. Credit cards and some loans compound interest, which is why a carried balance can balloon. Understanding this cuts both ways: it's a reason to invest early <em>and</em> to pay down high-interest debt aggressively.</p>

      <p>To see exactly how your own money could grow — including with regular monthly contributions — use the compound interest calculator below. It shows the full year-by-year breakdown.</p>
      <p><a href="/finance/compound-interest-calculator">Calculate your compound interest →</a></p>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>What is the rule of 72?</h3>
      <p>The rule of 72 is a quick way to estimate how long it takes money to double: divide 72 by your annual return. At 7%, money doubles roughly every 10 years (72 ÷ 7 ≈ 10).</p>
      <h3>Does more frequent compounding make a big difference?</h3>
      <p>For most everyday accounts, the difference between monthly and daily compounding is modest — often fractions of a percent per year. The rate and the time horizon matter far more.</p>
      <h3>Is compound interest taxable?</h3>
      <p>In a regular (taxable) account, interest and investment gains are generally taxable. Tax-advantaged accounts like IRAs and 401(k)s defer or eliminate that tax, which lets compounding work even faster.</p>

      <p class="privacy-note">For education only, not financial advice. Actual returns vary and are not guaranteed.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
